<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludovico Sforza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1452–1508</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ludovico il Moro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludovico Sforza, called il Moro, ruled the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> as regent from 1480 and as duke from 1494 to 1499. A great patron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, he employed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>, who painted the Last Supper and planned an equestrian monument for him. His encouragement of the French invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1494 backfired: Louis XII took Milan in 1499, and Ludovico died a prisoner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza.jpg" alt="Ludovico Sforza" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Master, Italian (active 1490-1520 in Lombardy) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pala_Sforzesca_-_detail_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
